The default SPI model uses a negative binomial distribution for counts and no additional explanatory variables (covariates). The negative binomial distribution allows counts to vary more than a Poisson distribution would. This article shows how to assess the effects of covariates, observation models, spatial structure, and the training period. These options apply to the model-based SPI (`spi_index()`), not to the simple SPI.

The examples use the bundled synthetic data. Model fitting requires INLA and can take several minutes.

```{r data, cache = FALSE}
library(spi)
synth <- synth_surveillance
```

## Adjusting for covariates

Covariates are optional and are not part of the default SPI used in the accompanying study. Adding covariates changes the expected counts and therefore what SPI compares. For example, including travel time makes the expected count depend partly on access to care. Pass district-year covariates to `spi_index()` or `spi_expected()` through the `covariates` argument. The example data include DTP3 coverage, urban population share, and travel time to care:

```{r covariates}
synth$covariates |>
  dplyr::slice_head(n = 6)
```

To inspect the covariate effects, fit the model once with `spi_expected()`, log-transforming the skewed travel-time column:

```{r fit-adjusted}
fit_adj <- spi_expected(
  cases = synth$cases,
  population = synth$population,
  adjacency = synth$boundaries,
  covariates = synth$covariates,
  log_transform = "travel_time_min",
  id_col = "adm2_guid",
  n_draws = 500,
  seed = 42,
  verbose = FALSE
)
```

Effects are reported as rate ratios for a one-standard-deviation increase in each covariate. The model first rescales each covariate to have mean 0 and standard deviation 1. Here are the three covariate effects, excluding the seasonal terms:

```{r fit-adjusted-show}
summary(fit_adj)$effects |>
  dplyr::filter(covariate %in% c("dtp3", "urban_prop", "travel_time_min")) |>
  dplyr::select(covariate, rr_median, rr_q025, rr_q975, signif)
```

These coefficients describe associations with reporting after accounting for the other model terms. They do not show that changing a covariate would cause reporting to change.

## Compare overdispersion options

Compare Poisson, Poisson with an observation-level random effect, and negative binomial models:

```{r overdispersion}
od <- spi_compare_overdispersion(
  cases = synth$cases,
  population = synth$population,
  adjacency = synth$boundaries,
  id_col = "adm2_guid",
  specs = c("none", "iid", "nb"),
  n_draws = 100,
  verbose = FALSE
)
```

```{r overdispersion-show}
od$summary
```

`od$summary` has one row per model. DIC (deviance information criterion) and WAIC (widely applicable information criterion) compare fit while allowing for model complexity; lower values are preferred. `cpo_valid` is the share of observations with a valid conditional predictive ordinate, a check of how well each observation is predicted when left out of the fit. `pit_ks` measures how far the probability integral transform (PIT) values are from a uniform distribution; smaller values indicate closer agreement with that reference.

`od$recommendation` gives the model selected by the package's diagnostic rule, with its reasoning:

```{r overdispersion-choice}
od$recommendation$choice
od$recommendation$reasoning
```

<details>
<summary>How the diagnostic rule selects a model</summary>

WAIC can favour a model with one random effect per observation even when that model overfits. `spi_compare_overdispersion()` therefore first excludes any model with valid CPO values for fewer than half of the observations, an effective number of parameters above 20% of the observations, or a BYM2 `phi` below 0.02 or above 0.98. Among the remaining models, it selects the one with the smallest PIT distance.

</details>

To skip the manual step, `spi_expected(overdispersion = "auto")` runs this same comparison internally and refits with the selected model. Here the diagnostic rule selects `r sprintf('\x60overdispersion = "%s"\x60', od$recommendation$choice)`. Negative binomial remains the package default because it is the pre-specified model used in the accompanying study; `overdispersion = "auto"` is an optional diagnostic workflow for other applications.

## Spatial structure

`spi_expected()` builds the neighbour graph itself when `adjacency` is an `sf` object. To inspect or reuse the graph, build it with `spi_adjacency()`:

```{r adjacency}
adj <- spi_adjacency(synth$boundaries, id_col = "adm2_guid")
adj
```

Districts are neighbours when their boundaries touch at any point (`contiguity = "queen"`); `contiguity = "rook"` requires a shared edge. With `handle_islands = TRUE`, the default, a district with no neighbours is linked to its nearest district by centroid distance. A group of connected districts that has no link to the rest remains separate. The printed graph calls these groups "disjoint connected subgraphs".

The spatial term is a BYM2 random effect (Riebler et al. 2016). It splits district-level variation into a spatially structured part, shared with neighbours, and an unstructured part specific to each district; `phi` is the share of that variation that is spatially structured. When a district has little data, the model uses information from other districts, including its neighbours, to help estimate its expected count. This makes the estimate more stable, but it can pull an unusual district towards its neighbours.

## Sensitivity checks

Results can depend on the training period, observation model, covariates, centring, and reporting year. Each check below calculates SPI for 2022 to 2024 with one setting changed, using 100 posterior draws to keep the run time down. Each check counts district-years that cover all 12 months and have an SPI below 1. The count is a simple illustration of how classification changes between settings, not a measure of model performance:

```{r sensitivity}
sensitivity_index <- function(...) {
  spi_index(
    cases = synth$cases,
    population = synth$population,
    adjacency = synth$boundaries,
    first_assessment = 2022,
    last_assessment = 2024,
    id_col = "adm2_guid",
    n_draws = 100,
    seed = 42,
    verbose = FALSE,
    ...
  )
}

count_below_1 <- function(index) {
  index |>
    tibble::as_tibble() |>
    dplyr::filter(n_months == 12) |>
    dplyr::summarise(
      district_years = dplyr::n(),
      below_1 = sum(spi_median < 1)
    )
}

dplyr::bind_rows(
  default = count_below_1(sensitivity_index()),
  centre_none = count_below_1(sensitivity_index(centre = "none")),
  may_to_april = count_below_1(sensitivity_index(year_end_month = 4)),
  covariates = count_below_1(sensitivity_index(
    covariates = synth$covariates,
    log_transform = "travel_time_min"
  )),
  poisson_iid = count_below_1(sensitivity_index(overdispersion = "iid")),
  .id = "setting"
)
```

Starting assessment in 2021 skips the earliest assessment years. It does not change the training history for years already included, because each fit still uses all preceding years:

```{r sensitivity-training, eval = FALSE}
spi_later <- spi_index(
  cases = synth$cases,
  population = synth$population,
  adjacency = synth$boundaries,
  first_assessment = 2021,
  id_col = "adm2_guid"
)
```

Keep the chosen settings the same when comparing years, and report them with the results.
